---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Landscape from '../components/Landscape.astro';
import type { Well } from '../scripts/landscape';
import { SITE } from '../site';

// One descent through everything, oldest on the left. Each project is a shallow basin; Nimbus is the deep one.
// `side` puts the label above or below its point, `align` keeps the ones near an edge on the map.
const stops = [
  { id: 'learning-to-walk', name: 'Learning to Walk', year: '2017', x: 0.06, y: 0.36, side: 'above', align: 'start' },
  { id: 'wysiwyg-ai', name: 'Wysiwyg AI', year: '2017', x: 0.17, y: 0.68, side: 'below' },
  { id: 'eschernode', name: 'Eschernode', year: '2020', x: 0.29, y: 0.3, side: 'above' },
  { id: 'compute-manager', name: 'Compute Manager', year: '2021', x: 0.4, y: 0.7, side: 'below' },
  { id: 'lookwise', name: 'Lookwise', year: '2025', x: 0.51, y: 0.3, side: 'above' },
  { id: 'fast-gst-refund', name: 'Fast GST Refund', year: '2026', x: 0.62, y: 0.7, side: 'below' },
  { id: 'floaty', name: 'Floaty', year: '2026', x: 0.72, y: 0.28, side: 'above' },
  { id: 'nimbus', name: 'Nimbus', year: 'now', x: 0.86, y: 0.58, side: 'below', align: 'end' },
];

const wells: Well[] = [
  ...stops.map(({ id, x, y }): Well =>
    id === 'nimbus' ? { x, y, depth: -1.25, spread: 0.2 } : { x, y, depth: -0.55, spread: 0.075 },
  ),
  { x: 0.12, y: 0.02, depth: 0.6, spread: 0.16 },
  { x: 0.33, y: 1, depth: 0.55, spread: 0.17 },
  { x: 0.6, y: 0, depth: 0.45, spread: 0.13 },
  { x: 0.98, y: 0.02, depth: 0.4, spread: 0.14 },
  { x: 0.46, y: 0.5, depth: 0.2, spread: 0.07 },
];

const linkedin = SITE.links.find(({ label }) => label === 'LinkedIn')!.href;
---

<Base
  title="Work"
  description="Anil Karaka's work: Nimbus, an AI browser that runs two small businesses, plus everything built on the way there."
>
  <div class="hero route-map">
    <Landscape seed="work" paths={0} wells={wells} route={stops.map(({ x, y }) => [x, y])} />
    <ol class="route-labels">
      {
        stops.map(({ id, name, year, x, y, side, align }) => (
          <li class:list={[side, align, { here: id === 'nimbus' }]} style={`--x: ${x * 100}%; --y: ${y * 100}%`}>
            <a href={`#${id}`}>
              <small>{year}</small> {name}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="page-head work-head">
    <h1 class="page-title">Anil Karaka</h1>
    <p>
      I build software, mostly on my own and lately mostly with agents. The red line up there is the run so far. Every
      ring is a project that was a good enough place to stop, for a while. The deepest basin is where I am now.
    </p>
    <p class="hint">Click the map to drop your own ball. It will roll into one of them.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="work-section">
    <h2>Now</h2>
    <div>
      <article class="project project--lead" id="nimbus">
        <header>
          <h3>Nimbus</h3>
          <p class="kicker">An AI browser with the UX of Claude Code</p>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            You say what you want done on the web. Nimbus plans it, opens the pages, fills in the forms, and stops to
            ask before anything it can't undo.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Guide mode.</strong> Ghost-text suggestions inside any form you are filling in. Tab to accept, keep
              typing to override.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Autonomous mode.</strong> Nimbus drives, and hands the wheel back at CAPTCHAs, logins, file uploads,
              and anything that needs your judgment.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>Mac, Windows and Linux. It's in alpha, and free for life for the first 500 founding users.</p>
          <pre><code>brew install --cask syllogismos/tap/nimbus</code></pre>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="https://usenimbus.app">usenimbus.app</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/nimbus/releases/latest">Download</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <div class="prose operator-intro">
        <h3>Why it exists: the operator surface</h3>
        <p>
          I run two small businesses, Fast GST Refund and Lookwise, and almost none of the work of running them is
          writing code. It's web dashboards: ad managers, App Store Connect, MCP directories, plugin marketplaces,
          partner portals. None of them have a decent API, all of them are repetitive, and they are exactly where coding
          agents stop.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nimbus is where that work happens. Both businesses are operated through it, AI-native from end to end, which
          also makes them its hardest and most honest test.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="operator" aria-label="Nimbus operates Fast GST Refund and Lookwise">
        <div class="operator-hub">
          <strong>Nimbus</strong>
          <span>one browser, one agent, every dashboard</span>
        </div>
        <div class="operator-arms">
          <a class="operator-node" href="#fast-gst-refund">
            <strong>Fast GST Refund</strong>
            <span>MCP directories, plugin marketplaces, guides</span>
          </a>
          <a class="operator-node" href="#lookwise">
            <strong>Lookwise</strong>
            <span>Reddit ads, App Store Connect</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </figure>

      <article class="project" id="fast-gst-refund">
        <header>
          <h3>Fast GST Refund</h3>
          <time>2026</time>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Indian exporters claiming a GST refund have to upload two JSON files, Statement 3 and Annexure B. The
            government expects you to make them with a pair of Windows Excel utilities. Fast GST Refund makes them in
            the browser: paste your records, fix the problems it lists, pay ₹499 once per GSTIN and refund period,
            download.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's a micro-SaaS, and an experiment in how much of a business can be AI-native, end to end. It was built in
            Claude Code sessions, and the transcripts are committed with the code. Its files are byte-identical to the
            official utilities', checked against a real filing the portal accepted. And it ships as an MCP server, so
            your AI assistant can check your records and make the files itself.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="https://fastgstrefund.com">fastgstrefund.com</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://fastgstrefund.com/for-agents/">Use it from your AI assistant</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="project" id="lookwise">
        <header>
          <h3>Lookwise</h3>
          <time>2025</time>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            AI color analysis and a daily outfit, on the iPhone. Find out which colors actually suit you, get outfit
            ideas for your body type and your week, and stop buying clothes in the wrong colors.
          </p>
          <p>
            My second fashion app. The <a href="/blog/2014/09/04/tinder-like-android-app-that-showcases-products-from-a-fashion-website/"
              >first</a
            > was Tinder for clothes, on Android, in 2014.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/lookwise-color-analysis/id6747015605">App Store</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-section">
    <h2>Before</h2>
    <div>
      <article class="project" id="floaty">
        <header>
          <h3>Floaty</h3>
          <time>2026</time>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The baby monitor, floating over whatever I'm working on. A native menu bar app for macOS that plays ONVIF and
            RTSP cameras on your local network in a small always-on-top window. Drag it, resize it, double-click to
            zoom. No cloud, no recording.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/floaty/releases/latest/download/Floaty-mac-arm64.dmg">Download</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/floaty">GitHub</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="project" id="compute-manager">
        <header>
          <h3>Compute Manager</h3>
          <time>2021</time>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The AWS console as a native Mac app. It shows what each EC2 instance really costs, attached EBS volumes
            included, starts, stops and terminates them, lists spot prices, and browses S3 with drag-and-drop uploads.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/compute-manager/id1579078660">Mac App Store</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/Compute-Manager">GitHub</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="project" id="eschernode">
        <header>
          <h3>Eschernode</h3>
          <time>2020</time>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Twitter DM automation. It downloads all your followers, even past a million, lets you filter them, and sends
            them targeted direct-message campaigns, with link clicks and conversions tracked per campaign.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="https://eschernode.com">eschernode.com</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/eschernode-dashboard">Dashboard</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/eschernode-backend">Backend</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="project" id="wysiwyg-ai">
        <header>
          <h3>Wysiwyg AI</h3>
          <time>2017</time>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Developer tools for machine learning, built right after the walking challenge below. Draw a neural network
            in an editor instead of writing it, launch supervised or reinforcement learning experiments on cloud GPUs,
            and track every run. Angular, Django, Celery and Elasticsearch. The backend README doubles as my notes on
            running all of that on AWS.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/wysiwyg-ai">Dashboard</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/wysiwyg-ai-backend">Backend and notes</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="project" id="learning-to-walk">
        <header>
          <h3>Learning to Walk</h3>
          <time>2017</time>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The reinforcement learning experiment that started it. A skeleton with 18 muscles, a physics engine, and five
            seconds to get as far as it can without falling over. My TRPO agent won crowdAI's Learning How to Walk
            challenge with almost 29 metres. Second and third place learned to hop. Mine learned to walk.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="/blog/2017/10/04/learning-to-walk/">Write-up</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/syllogismos/modular_rl_crowdai">Code</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-section">
    <h2>Jobs</h2>
    <div>
      <article class="project">
        <header>
          <h3>Zenskar</h3>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Billing and revenue automation for B2B companies.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="project">
        <header>
          <h3>MoEngage</h3>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            One of the early engineers at the marketing automation company. I built segmentation from the ground up on
            Elasticsearch: which of an app's users match a marketer's rule, fast enough to message them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul class="project-links">
          <li><a href="/blog/2017/07/30/elasticsearch-segmentation-moengage/">Write-up</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <p class="work-more">Titles, dates and the rest are on <a href={linkedin}>LinkedIn</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
